window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][15]={"university_physics_en:M15-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M15-L1 – Nuclear Structure, Isotopes and Binding Energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Mass and energy, atomic models,\nelectrostatics, and introductory quantum physics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify nuclides using A and Z\nand connect the mass defect to binding energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nuclide Notation and Nuclear Forces<\/h2>\n<p>A nuclide is written\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{A}_{Z}X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAtomic number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts protons, mass number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=Z+N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts all nucleons, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the neutron count. Isotopes have identical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut different\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nProtons repel electrically; a predominantly attractive short-range\nresidual strong interaction nevertheless binds many nuclei. Its range is\nof order a few femtometres and it is not a simple Coulomb potential.\nNuclear radii are approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=r_0A^{1/3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabout\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\,\\mathrm{fm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis empirical scaling indicates roughly constant nucleon density, not a\nhard surface or a precise radius for every nucleus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mass Defect and Binding Energy<\/h2>\n<p>A bound nucleus is less massive than its free constituent nucleons.\nWith nuclear masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_p,m_n,m_{\\mathrm{nucleus}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mass defect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=Zm_p+Nm_n-m_{\\mathrm{nucleus}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe energy needed to separate all nucleons is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\Delta m\\,c^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>When neutral atomic masses are used instead, one may calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=Zm(^{1}H)+Nm_n-m_{\\mathrm{atom}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nelectron rest masses approximately cancel, though electronic\nbinding-energy differences matter at high accuracy. Binding energy per\nnucleon\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a useful stability indicator but does not alone decide whether a\ndecay can occur. Possible final states must also satisfy conservation\nlaws and interaction dynamics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Helium-4<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuse approximate atomic masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.007825<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(^{1}H)=1.007825\\,\\mathrm{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.008665<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_n=1.008665\\,\\mathrm{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.002603<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(^{4}He)=4.002603\\,\\mathrm{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mass defect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.007825<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.008665<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4.002603<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030377<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=2(1.007825)+2(1.008665)-4.002603=0.030377\\,\\mathrm{u}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>931.494<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{u}\\,c^2\\approx931.494\\,\\mathrm{MeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>28.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.07<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx28.30\\,\\mathrm{MeV},\\qquad B/A\\approx7.07\\,\\mathrm{MeV}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The diagram shows nucleons and the energy difference between\nseparated and bound states. It does not portray literal classical orbits\nfor individual nucleons.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_192_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four separated nucleons and a bound helium nucleus; energy difference marked as binding energy\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four separated nucleons and a bound\nhelium nucleus; energy difference marked as binding energy<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Binding lowers the total rest energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many neutrons does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{14}_{6}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontain?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=0.010\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone not determine a decay rate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=A-Z=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.315<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx9.315\\,\\mathrm{MeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Conservation laws, barriers and transition matrix elements control\nchannels and rates.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The liquid-drop model combines volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry\nand pairing contributions. It explains broad systematics but not the\ndetailed nuclear shell structure. Particularly stable proton and neutron\ncounts reflect quantized nuclear levels and spin–orbit coupling rather\nthan average binding energy alone. Actual nuclei can be excited,\ndeformed or short-lived. A spontaneous reaction needs positive available\nenergy in an allowed channel; an energetically possible process is not\nnecessarily fast. Atomic and nuclear masses must be distinguished in\nhigh-precision calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Mass defect and binding energy describe the same mass difference;\nstability also depends on dynamics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Radioactive Decay and\nHalf-Life<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 10 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–34; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L1 – Nuclear Structure, Isotopes and Binding Energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-2-nuclear-binding-energy\">OpenStax – Nuclear Binding Energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 10.4, lines 32–39, 58–63; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L1 – Nuclear Structure, Isotopes and Binding Energy<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M15-L2 – Radioactive Decay and Half-Life<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M11: quantum states; M13:\nprobability; M15-L1: nuclides. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the decay law and\nactivity and interpret statistical statements correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random Decay and Decay Modes<\/h2>\n<p>Radioactive decay is a quantum transition of an unstable nuclear\nstate. Alpha decay emits a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnucleus; beta-minus decay converts a neutron into a proton while\nemitting an electron and an electron antineutrino. An excited nucleus\nmay emit a gamma photon without changing its mass number. The decay time\nof an individual nucleus cannot be predicted from how long it has\nalready existed. In an ensemble of identical independent nuclei with\ntime-independent decay constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability of decay in a short time interval is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen several decay channels exist, their partial decay constants add to\nthe total\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Equation and Activity<\/h2>\n<p>The expected remaining count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dN/dt=-\\lambda N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSeparation and integration yield<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=N_0e^{-\\lambda t},\\qquad T_{1/2}=\\frac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Activity is the mean number of decays per second:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t)=-dN/dt=\\lambda N(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmeasured in becquerels,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Bq}=1\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalf-life does not depend on the initial amount. Mean lifetime is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDaughter products in a radioactive chain generally require coupled\ndifferential equations rather than a single independent exponential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two Half-Lives<\/h2>\n<p>Take half-life\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}=5.0\\,\\mathrm{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand initial activity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=800\\,\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10.0\\,\\mathrm{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexactly two half-lives have elapsed:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t)=A_0 2^{-t/T_{1/2}}=200\\,\\mathrm{Bq}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The decay constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1386<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=\\ln 2/(5\\,\\mathrm h)\\approx0.1386\\,\\mathrm{h^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graph shows normalized activity dropping to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter one, two and three half-lives. These are ensemble expectations;\nobserved counts from a weak source fluctuate around their mean.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_066_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential decay with marked half-lives and remaining fractions\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential decay with marked half-lives\nand remaining fractions<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Expected count N/N0 = 2^(−t/T½)<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter three half-lives.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}=2\\,\\mathrm d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the ideal counting uncertainty for 100 events?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8=0.125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3466<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.3466\\,\\mathrm{d^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts, relative uncertainty about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counting Statistics and Model Limits<\/h2>\n<p>Independent rare detected events approximately follow Poisson\nstatistics over a fixed observation window. If the expected count is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbefore background corrections and under idealized detection. Efficiency,\ndead time, background, shielding and physical losses influence\nmeasurements. A long half-life does not automatically mean small\nactivity at every sample mass or low biological hazard. Exponential\ndecay assumes no replenishment and a constant decay rate.\nIrradiation-induced nuclear reactions should be distinguished from\nspontaneous decay.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Decay law describes ensemble expectations; activity and registered\nrate differ for real detectors.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Nuclear Reactions, Q Value and\nThreshold<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 10 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–34; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L2 – Radioactive Decay and Half-Life · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay\">OpenStax – Radioactive Decay<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 10.12–10.18, lines 70–105; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L2 – Radioactive Decay and Half-Life<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M15-L3 – Nuclear Reactions, Q Value and Threshold<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15-L1, energy and momentum\nconservation, overview of relativity. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute reaction Q values from\nmasses and distinguish energetic allowance from reaction rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reaction Channels and Nucleon Balance<\/h2>\n<p>In nuclear reactions a projectile and target scatter or transform\ninto products. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+A\\rightarrow b+B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes projectile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlaboratory target\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand outgoing particles\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b,B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a two-body channel. Total electric charge and baryon number are\nconserved in ordinary nuclear reactions, even when weak transformations\nredistribute protons and neutrons. Energy resides in rest mass, motion\nand internal excitation. Examples include neutron capture, heavy-nucleus\nfission and light-nucleus fusion. Nuclear reactions differ from chemical\nchanges: rearrangements of nuclear binding typically involve much larger\nenergies than electronic bonds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Q Value and Conservation Laws<\/h2>\n<p>Reaction Q value is the difference between total initial and final\nrest energies:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\left(\\sum m_{\\mathrm{initial}}-\\sum m_{\\mathrm{final}}\\right)c^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For nonrelativistic kinetic energies and ground-state products,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{\\mathrm{final}}=K_{\\mathrm{initial}}+Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPositive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis exoenergetic; negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis endoenergetic. Momentum conservation and possible Coulomb barriers\nremain important even for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\na collision needs the appropriate energy and interaction. When atomic\nmasses are used, electron numbers and masses must be handled\nconsistently. Excited products reduce the kinetic energy available by\nthe excitation energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Fusion Energy<\/h2>\n<p>The reaction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2H+{}^3H\\rightarrow{}^4He+n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas approximate atomic masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.014102<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_D=2.014102\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.016049<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_T=3.016049\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.002603<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_{He}=4.002603\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.008665<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_n=1.008665\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mass difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.018883<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=0.018883\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>17.59<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx17.59\\,\\mathrm{MeV}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>By momentum conservation most of the released energy goes to the\nneutron and some to the helium nucleus. This number says nothing about\nthe net engineering energy balance of a reactor: ignition, confinement,\nlosses and materials are separate problems. The diagram compares total\nrest energies before and after the reaction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_239_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Energy bar chart showing initial and final total rest energies for fusion with Q value\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Energy bar chart showing initial and\nfinal total rest energies for fusion with Q value<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>D + T → He-4 + n; Q ≈ 17.59 MeV<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What sign does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave when final rest mass is larger?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=0.002\\,\\mathrm u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot imply a large reaction cross section?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Negative: endoenergetic.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.863<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx1.863\\,\\mathrm{MeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Coulomb barriers, selection rules and weak matrix elements can\nsuppress a process.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Thresholds and Physical Limits<\/h2>\n<p>For endoenergetic reactions on a stationary target, the laboratory\nprojectile threshold generally exceeds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin magnitude because the products carry collective momentum. Exact\nrelativistic thresholds follow from invariant energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(p_a+p_A)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nResonances, angular momentum, nuclear spin, tunnelling and target\nisotopes influence measured reaction rates. Fission and fusion are not\ninterchangeable reverse processes operating at one energy; their\nchannels and barriers differ. A positive Q value does not guarantee a\nprompt or wholly usable energy release.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Q value is a rest-energy difference; thresholds and rates also depend\non kinematics and dynamics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Interaction of Ionizing\nRadiation with Matter<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 10 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–34; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L3 – Nuclear Reactions, Q Value and Threshold · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M15-L4 – Interaction of Ionizing Radiation with Matter<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M11-L2, M15-L2, exponential decay and\nenergy conservation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish photon attenuation\nfrom charged-particle tracks and calculate a simple shielding model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Photons and Charged Particles<\/h2>\n<p>X-ray and gamma photons are uncharged. They transfer energy through\nphotoelectric absorption, Compton scattering and pair production.\nDominance depends on photon energy and material; pair creation in a\nnuclear Coulomb field requires at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2m_ec^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin photon energy. Charged alpha particles, electrons and protons instead\nlose energy mainly through many electromagnetic collisions and\nionizations. Their ranges and tracks differ considerably. A claim about\npenetration requires particle type, energy and target material.\nRadiation protection must distinguish source activity, radiation field,\ndeposited energy and biological effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Narrow-Beam Exponential Attenuation<\/h2>\n<p>For a narrow, monoenergetic photon beam in which only unscattered\nprimary photons are counted,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dI/dx=-\\mu I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a useful model. Its solution is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(x)=I_0e^{-\\mu x},\\qquad x_{1/2}=\\frac{\\ln2}{\\mu}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The linear attenuation coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nNIST often tabulates mass attenuation coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu/\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=(\\mu/\\rho)\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a homogeneous material. Attenuation removes photons from the primary\nbeam but is not synonymous with complete energy absorption. Broad-beam\ngeometry admits scattered photons and needs build-up and transport\nmodels. Coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvaries strongly with photon energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Half-Value Layer<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.50\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe half-value layer is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.386<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}=\\ln2/0.50\\approx1.386\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA plate of thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransmits the ideal narrow-beam primary fraction<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.223<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=e^{-1.5}\\approx0.223.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The figure plots exponential primary-beam attenuation in a uniform\nslab and marks the half-value layer. It is not a clinical dose\ndistribution. To convert NIST mass attenuation values into geometrical\nlength, material density must be included. A mixture of energies and\nscattered components cannot generally be represented by one universal\nattenuation factor.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_012_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential primary intensity with half-value layer and slab cross section\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential primary intensity with\nhalf-value layer and slab cross section<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal narrow primary beam: I/I0 = exp(−μx)<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2x_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1/2}=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is half-value layer not a dose value?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3466<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\approx0.3466\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It describes removal from a specified primary beam; dose depends on\nenergy deposition in the material.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dose, Scattering and Limits<\/h2>\n<p>Absorbed dose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis deposited energy per mass,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=1\\,\\mathrm{J/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nequivalent and effective dose additionally use biological weighting\nfactors and are quoted in sievert. Gray and sievert must not be casually\nequated. Radiation effects depend on energy, exposure configuration and\ntissue; practical protection follows established radiation-safety\nstandards rather than an attenuation toy model. Electrons may produce\nbremsstrahlung, and neutrons need different shielding models.\nMeasurements also depend on background, geometry, efficiency and\nspectral response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Attenuation, activity and dose are different quantities; exponential\ntransmission needs precise geometrical assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Scattering and Differential\nCross Section<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 10 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–34; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L4 – Interaction of Ionizing Radiation with Matter · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/PhysRefData/XrayMassCoef/chap2.html\">NIST – X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients §2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Eq. 1–2, lines 3–12, 19–24; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L4 – Interaction of Ionizing Radiation with Matter<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M15-L5 – Scattering and Differential Cross Section<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: scattering theory, M15-L3,\nspherical coordinates and wave interference. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret cross section as rate\nper incoming flux and apply elastic scattering amplitudes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Event Rate and Scattering Geometry<\/h2>\n<p>Scattering is characterized by observed event counts, incident beam\nproperties and the detector’s angular acceptance. Total cross section\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas dimensions of area. It is not necessarily the physical outline of a\npoint particle but a measure of reaction probability. For a thin target\nwith areal density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand incident particle rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot N_{\\mathrm{in}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na single-scattering approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot N_{\\mathrm{sc}}\\approx\\dot N_{\\mathrm{in}}n_T\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nColliding beams instead use luminosity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\mathcal L\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcceptance, detection efficiency, multiple scattering and background\nmust be considered independently in practice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Cross Section<\/h2>\n<p>The angular distribution is defined by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}=\\frac{d\\dot N_{\\mathrm{sc}}/d\\Omega}{j_{\\mathrm{in}}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For nonrelativistic elastic potential scattering with asymptotic wave\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi\\sim e^{ikz}+f(\\theta,\\phi)e^{ikr}/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{d\\sigma}{d\\Omega}=|f(\\theta,\\phi)|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Scattering amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas dimensions of length. In general, kinematic factors, spins, multiple\nchannels, particle identities and phase space must be included. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a universal inelastic relativistic scattering formula. Total\ncross section follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\int(d\\sigma/d\\Omega)\\,d\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the physically allowed angular domain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Isotropic Scattering<\/h2>\n<p>Consider an ideal elastic scattering process isotropic over all solid\nangles with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sigma/d\\Omega=2.0\\,\\mathrm{mb/sr}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int d\\Omega=4\\pi\\,\\mathrm{sr}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>25.13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=4\\pi\\,(2.0\\,\\mathrm{mb/sr})\\approx25.13\\,\\mathrm{mb}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An angular bin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\Omega=0.10\\,\\mathrm{sr}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to an integrated bin cross section of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{mb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic labels incident beam, target, scattering angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a small detector acceptance region. It illustrates angles, not\nmeasured nuclear data. The unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>28<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm b=10^{-28}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis customary in nuclear and particle physics.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_257_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Incident beam, scattering center, angle theta and sector-like detector acceptance\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Incident beam, scattering center, angle\ntheta and sector-like detector acceptance<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>An angular bin selects part of the cross section.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor isotropic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sigma/d\\Omega=1\\,\\mathrm{mb/sr}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Express\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin SI area units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sigma/d\\Omega=|f|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalid without extra factors?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>12.57<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=4\\pi\\,\\mathrm{mb}\\approx12.57\\,\\mathrm{mb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the stated nonrelativistic elastic potential-scattering limit\nwith consistent flux normalization.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Corrections<\/h2>\n<p>Scattering need not be isotropic. Coulomb scattering can be strongly\nforward-peaked, and identical particles require symmetrized or\nantisymmetrized amplitudes. Detectors usually measure folded\ndistributions: resolution, angular acceptance and trigger selection\naffect the raw histogram. Finite target thickness brings absorption and\nmultiple interactions. Luminosity in a collider has units of inverse\narea per time and must not be confused with cross section. One millibarn\nequals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Cross sections connect rates and fluxes; amplitude formulas apply\nonly under their specific assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Elementary Particles and the\nStandard Model<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 11 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–35; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L5 – Scattering and Differential Cross Section · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-06-quantum-physics-iii-spring-2018/45dbd7038c3e969491eceeae86c44d42_MIT8_06S18ch7.pdf\">MIT OpenCourseWare – MIT OCW, Quantum Physics III, Chapter 7: Scattering (Spring 2018)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 136, PDF page 4, Eq. 7.1.10–7.1.14; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L5 – Scattering and Differential Cross Section<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M15-L6 – Elementary Particles and the Standard Model<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Quantum mechanics, electrodynamics,\nM15-L5 and relativistic energy basics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Classify fermion generations,\ngauge bosons and the Higgs field in the Standard Model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Matter Fields in Three Generations<\/h2>\n<p>The Standard Model contains fundamental fermions arranged in three\ngenerations. Each generation contains two quark flavours and a charged\nlepton with its neutrino; corresponding antiparticles exist. Quarks\ncarry color charge and at low energies usually appear inside hadrons. A\nproton has two up and one down valence quarks, a neutron one up and two\ndown. The valence description does not erase gluon and sea-quark\ncontributions to proton structure in quantum field theory. Neutrinos\nhave no electric charge and participate in weak interactions and\ngravity, the latter not being part of the Standard Model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interactions and Gauge Bosons<\/h2>\n<p>The model covers electromagnetic, weak and strong interactions. The\nphoton couples to electric charge;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>W<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W^\\pm<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z^0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmediate weak processes, while eight gluons transmit color interactions\namong quarks. Electromagnetism and the weak force are unified in\nelectroweak theory. The Higgs field has a nonzero vacuum expectation\nvalue and its couplings contribute to masses of elementary fermions and\nweak gauge bosons. Much of the mass of composite hadrons instead arises\nfrom QCD dynamics. The quantized Higgs field has an observed scalar\nexcitation, the Higgs boson. It is inaccurate to say that every particle\nreceives all of its mass through one simple mechanism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Quark Electric Charge<\/h2>\n<p>Up- and down-quark electric charges are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>e<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_u=+2e/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_d=-e/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the valence picture, proton and neutron charges are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_p=2Q_u+Q_d=+e,\\qquad Q_n=Q_u+2Q_d=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Electric charge is additive and conserved. The figure tabulates three\nfermion generations and gauge-boson classes; cell sizes do not represent\nrelative particle masses. Proton mass is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>938<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">938\\,\\mathrm{MeV}/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cannot be obtained by adding small current-quark masses alone.\nBinding and field dynamics dominate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_275_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Table of quarks and leptons by generation plus gauge bosons and Higgs\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Table of quarks and leptons by generation\nplus gauge bosons and Higgs<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Three fermion generations and mediated interactions<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the proton valence-quark charge in units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which three interactions are in the Standard Model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the proton mass not explained solely by the Higgs field?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2/3+2/3-1/3=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Electromagnetic, weak and strong.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Much arises from QCD quark–gluon dynamics.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope of the Standard Model<\/h2>\n<p>The Standard Model is highly successful but not a complete theory of\nall known phenomena. It does not describe gravity, does not identify\ndark matter and does not derive its measured parameters from one simple\nprinciple. Neutrino oscillations establish nonzero neutrino masses and\nrequire an extension beyond the minimal massless-neutrino formulation.\nIts language is quantum fields and interactions: a table of colored\nblocks must not be interpreted as a mechanical assembly diagram of\nelectrons or Higgs bosons. Experimental uncertainties and model\ndependence remain part of every physical claim.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Standard Model explains matter fields and three interactions; its\nopen questions must be distinguished from tested predictions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Conservation Laws and Particle\nTransformations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.web.cern.ch/science/physics/standard-model/\">CERN – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 80–93; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L6 – Elementary Particles and the Standard Model · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-introduction\">University Physics III – Chapter 11 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 22–35; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L6 – Elementary Particles and the Standard Model · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M15-L7 – Conservation Laws and Particle Transformations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15-L6, special relativity and\nelementary quantum mechanics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Balance energy, momentum and\ncharge in particle reactions and identify interaction-dependent\nquantum-number rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spacetime Conservation Laws<\/h2>\n<p>Energy and momentum combine into a conserved four-momentum. For\ninitial and final particle four-momenta\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i^\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{\\mathrm{in}}p_i^\\mu=\\sum_{\\mathrm{out}}p_i^\\mu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Individual particle mass is Lorentz-invariant and obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sum of rest masses need not be unchanged: kinetic energy can become\nrest mass and vice versa. Total electric charge is also conserved in\nknown reactions. Angular momentum, including spin, must be accounted for\nacross all final states. An apparent violation of a selection rule\ncannot be inferred from an incompletely reconstructed event alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Charge, Baryon Number and Flavour<\/h2>\n<p>In ordinary observed processes baryon number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conserved: baryons carry\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nantibaryons\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLepton numbers are useful bookkeeping devices, but separate\nlepton-flavour numbers are not exactly conserved because neutrinos\noscillate. Strangeness is conserved in strong and electromagnetic\ninteractions but may change in weak processes. Allowed channels must\nalso obey energy availability, charge and other relevant quantum\nnumbers. Identifying the interaction is essential: a selection rule for\nstrong scattering need not apply to beta decay. Claims about\nconservation of baryon or lepton number must specify their theoretical\ndomain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Beta-Minus Transformation<\/h2>\n<p>Beta-minus decay converts a neutron into a proton, electron and\nelectron antineutrino:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>ν<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\rightarrow p+e^-+\\bar\\nu_e.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Charge is zero on the left and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+e-e+0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the right. Proton and neutron carry baryon number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin Standard Model bookkeeping; electron and antineutrino have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe produced electron has electron-flavour lepton number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe antineutrino\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving a vanishing sum in the usual production-vertex description. The\nfigure shows a particle accounting diagram, not the internal mechanism\nwithin the nucleon. Beta decay proceeds through the weak\ninteraction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_050_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Neutron transforming to proton, electron and antineutrino with total electric charge zero\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Neutron transforming to proton, electron\nand antineutrino with total electric charge zero<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>n → p + e− + ν̄e<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the total charge of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>ν<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\to p+e^-+\\bar\\nu_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproducts?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the relation connecting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is strangeness necessarily conserved in weak decay?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the weak interaction may change strangeness.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of Apparently Exact Rules<\/h2>\n<p>Free neutron beta decay is energetically allowed; within a nucleus,\nthe corresponding conversion may be forbidden by the full nuclear mass\nbalance. Electron capture can occur when total energies permit it.\nNeutrinos frequently leave a detector without visible tracks, causing\nmissing reconstructed momentum; such an inference must be checked\nagainst resolution and other unmeasured particles. Saying that a quantum\nnumber is “conserved” does not automatically mean exact in every\npossible new theory. Some symmetries are broken or approximate in\nextensions of the Standard Model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Conservation laws constrain reactions; flavour selection rules must\nnot be confused with universal symmetries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Accelerators, Particle Detectors\nand Event Reconstruction<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.web.cern.ch/science/physics/standard-model/\">CERN – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 80–93; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L7 – Conservation Laws and Particle Transformations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-2-particle-conservation-laws\">OpenStax – Particle Conservation Laws<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 30, 93–109, 129–151; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L7 – Conservation Laws and Particle Transformations<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M15-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M15-L8 – Accelerators, Particle Detectors and Event Reconstruction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Nuclear and Particle Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: Lorentz force, M15-L5–L7,\nfour-momentum and counting statistics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine acceleration, track\ncurvature and calorimetric energy measurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Acceleration and Beam Guidance<\/h2>\n<p>Electric fields transfer energy to charged particles; magnetic fields\nideally change direction at fixed particle energy. In a ring\naccelerator, radiofrequency cavities repeatedly accelerate particles,\ndipole magnets bend the orbit and quadrupole magnets focus the beam.\nColliding beams often provide more usable center-of-mass energy than a\nstationary target for given beam energies. Event rate is the product of\nluminosity and process cross section. Beam quality, bunch geometry and\nstability are crucial practical factors. An accelerator diagram\ndescribes principles rather than current operating energies or powers of\nany named facility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tracking, Calorimetry and Muon Systems<\/h2>\n<p>General-purpose detectors often combine inner trackers,\nelectromagnetic and hadronic calorimeters, and outer muon systems. In a\nuniform magnetic field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith transverse motion,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T=|q|Br<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn convenient units,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T[\\mathrm{GeV}/c]\\approx0.300\\,|z|\\,B[\\mathrm T]r[\\mathrm m],<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=ze<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTrack curvature gives charge sign and momentum; calorimeters record\ndeposited energy, while muons often traverse substantial material.\nNeutrinos are inferred indirectly from momentum balance and event\ntopology rather than directly tracked. Every detector has finite\nresolution and limited acceptance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Momentum from Curvature<\/h2>\n<p>A singly charged particle,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nin a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=2.0\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfield with idealized radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.300<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T\\approx0.300\\times2.0\\times1.5=0.90\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At fixed field and charge, higher momentum corresponds to a larger\nradius. The graphic shows a curved track, a calorimeter layer and an\nouter penetrating-muon path. The given full-circle radius is a model\nquantity; in real detectors short track segments must be fitted while\naccounting for material effects. Momentum resolution often deteriorates\nfor nearly straight tracks.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_202_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic detector layers with charged curved track and outer muon track\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic detector layers with charged\ncurved track and outer muon track<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Track curvature, calorimetry and muon identification<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=1.0\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a larger curvature radius imply at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does missing momentum not prove a specific new particle?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T\\approx0.60\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Larger transverse momentum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Neutrinos, unmeasured particles and detector effects can mimic the\nsignature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reconstruction and Uncertainties<\/h2>\n<p>Reconstruction combines calibration, geometrical alignment, timing,\ntrack fits, clustering and identification criteria. Multiple scattering,\nenergy loss, pile-up and detector dead time can bias measurements.\nTriggers reduce recorded event volume and their selection probabilities\nmust be modeled later. Missing transverse momentum indicates unseen or\nunmeasured momentum carriers, not proof of one particular new particle.\nAnalysis requires statistical and systematic uncertainties and\nindependent validation, not merely attractive event displays.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Accelerators produce controlled collisions; track and energy\nmeasurements require calibrated reconstruction to yield credible\nevents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/accelerators\">CERN – Accelerators<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · section How does an accelerator work?, lines 82–89; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L8 – Accelerators, Particle Detectors and Event Reconstruction · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/experiments/\">CERN – Experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 75–78, 102–105; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L8 – Accelerators, Particle Detectors and Event Reconstruction · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/experiments/how-detector-works\">CERN – How a detector works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 73–89; Subject relevance to lesson: M15-L8 – Accelerators, Particle Detectors and Event Reconstruction<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
